# The release currently RUNNING in production — the runtime a new release's
# migrations must stay backward-compatible with.
#
# A migration is applied at deploy step 10 and the runtime switches at step 14,
# so for the minutes in between THIS commit's code is reading the new schema.
# `tests/schema-compat.test.ts` checks out this commit and runs its own table
# definitions against the schema the candidate release produces.
#
# This is the deployed RUNTIME, not the newest commit that has been used for
# anything. The chain so far is aa1dbf5 from the Phase A deployment, then
# 5999109, then bae53b2, then 91ec445 — each written here only once it was live
# and verified. 93ec735 was never a runtime at all: it added the TGA cutover
# repair and the service restructure was run from an isolated 93ec735 worktree,
# but the web runtime was never switched to it. Recording a commit that merely
# ran a script would check compatibility against code nobody is serving.
#
# Update it as part of each release, after that release's runtime is live. Git
# cannot infer it: `HEAD~1` is the previous commit, not the previous release, a
# release is usually several commits, and a commit can be used without being
# deployed — as 93ec735 was.
#
# Not to be confused with LEGACY_REF in tests/helpers/env.ts, which is the
# pre-restructure historical fixture (ea20a22) and stays where it is.
#
# First non-comment line is the commit. Override for a one-off run with
# COMPAT_REF=<ref> npm test.
